From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LosDaemons13 <esteveloris@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:09:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nfp: virtual amiibo the front end places on the host's NFC
 reader

The host had no NFC reader, and its nfp hooks answered "tag not found" to
everything, so no game's amiibo feature could be used. A game reads only
an amiibo's ID from the tag (character, variant, figure type, model number
and series), the tag's UID and its own application area; the ID is public,
so a figure can be made from it with no dump of a real one and no key.

The reader now holds a virtual amiibo that the front end places by its ID
and name (dm_amiibo_place, front.h). The game finds it at its search (the
activate event, or GetDeviceState when it polls), mounts it and reads its
tag info (a stable 7-byte NXP UID derived from the ID), model info, common
info and register info (the default Mii, the name as nickname). When the
game stops detection after reading it, the amiibo leaves the reader, as a
figure lifted after a tap; dm_amiibo_remove takes it off and signals the
deactivate event. Application areas are kept per access ID under
DM_AMIIBO_DIR (an amiibo folder beside DM_SAVE by default), so one virtual
amiibo holds every game's data at once. The amiibo settings applets answer
as cancelled. Hooks that touch files keep their paths on the heap: a game
thread's stack can be 8 KB.

t_nfc.c runs a figure through the whole sequence: placed, found, its tag
and model, its area created, written, flushed and read back after a new
mount, then lifted and taken off.

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: amiibo features in every game that uses them (Super Mario Odyssey's costumes, invincibility, hearts and Power Moon hints first)
Issue: none yet (LosDaemons13/NX2Apple, amiibo in Super Mario Odyssey)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
